Copilot said:
No. Changing the model path does not prevent Uvicorn from loading.

Your current error is:



model = joblib.load("/root/ai-student-management/ml/placement_model.pkl")
Inside the Docker container:



WORKDIR /app
so Docker is looking for:



/root/ai-student-management/ml/placement_model.pkl
which does not exist.

Using:



model = joblib.load("ml/placement_model.pkl")
simply tells Python to load the model from:



/app/ml/placement_model.pkl
which is the correct location if your project structure is:



ai-student-management/
├── app/
│   └── main.py
├── ml/
│   └── placement_model.pkl
├── requirements.txt
└── Dockerfile
First verify the file exists
Run:



find . -name placement_model.pkl
Expected:



./ml/placement_model.pkl
If nothing is returned, the model file is missing and changing the path won't help.

Better approach
In app/main.py:



import os
import joblib
 
model_path = os.path.join("ml", "placement_model.pkl")
model = joblib.load(model_path)
After changing the code
You must rebuild the image:



docker rm test-student-app -f
docker rmi student-app:v1 -f
 
docker build --no-cache -t student-app:v1 .
Then run:



docker run -d --name test-student-app -p 8001:8001 student-app:v1
Check if file is really present
Please run:



find . -name placement_model.pkl
and



ls -l ml/
If placement_model.pkl is not there, that's the real issue and we need to recreate the model file. If it is there, changing the path and rebuilding will fix the startup error.





